think My 20260917.020101 lap landed tools/fixtures/b/backtick_path_scan.sh over Markdown alone and wrote its remainder on its own face: the same promise stands in a .rish witness header, a .kyri roster comment and a .rye module head, read by nothing. The card carried the open question -- one lap per language, or one meter over every authored comment.
think Answered with one meter. A rule written six times is a rule six files may come to disagree about, which is the tree's own repeated finding.
think Checked the absence half before building: tools/fixtures/c/comment_citation_scan.sh already reads Markdown LINK targets on comment lines of non-prose files, and its own header reads a backticked span PAST by name as one of four cases. So the two guards are the target half and the anchor half of one promise, the division tracked_link and link_text_promise already make for pages.
think Claimed the three paths on construction/fleet-claims.kyri and pushed the claim before writing a byte, under the baton's new-instrument clause.
obs The finding is that this population is not the Markdown one. In a prose page a backticked path is nearly always a citation; in a scan or a control it is as often a description of a room the script itself builds in a throwaway pen. A fourth genre the Markdown reading never needed.
obs Told apart by a checkable question rather than by judgment -- is the span's parent directory one this tree carries? That splits 113 spans into 61 living promises and 52 pen rooms.
obs Its honest limit, stated on the scan's face: the question reads whether a directory exists, so a pen path whose parent coincides with a real directory reads as a citation and sits in the ratchet.
obs Two mutations read silent on the first control run and both were honest -- my plants sat in a pen room, so removing a check moved them between two reported classes and never touched the gate. A mutation proven on a reported number alone cannot tell a sharpened report from a loosened gate.
obs The field test genuinely only sharpens the report rather than holding the refusal, and the scan says so on its own face rather than letting a reader assume otherwise. Its control leg asserts the gate stays where it was.
obs Reds-first inside the lap: the meter found two real stale citations on its first run and both were repaired by reading the running path rather than the prose.
obs The root walk asks for tools/fixtures and construction, both tracked, so these three files add no instance to REDS %788's 189-file sweep.
